 "figures": {
  "room": {
   "title": "What a best reply would gain, era by era",
   "caption": "Each line is a family of long worlds: for a sample of up to 300 agents of each world holding three tokens or more, the nodes it would hold after one game by its best reply to the others' play, less what its own play holds, averaged. A shaded band, where a family has several worlds, runs from its lowest world to its highest; the line is the median. A population at an equilibrium would score 0.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** The lab's long worlds: Experiment 18's two worlds played to 50,000 iterations (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s005`, `-s006`, every 2,500), Experiment 19's ten to 20,000 (`B2-10000-bcc1bc-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), Experiment 17's to 10,000 (`B2-10000-4af4a3-s001` \u2026 `-s010` but the two above) and Experiment 16's B2 worlds to 2,000 (`B2-10000-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), at the iterations their archives keep.\n\n**Data.** From the worlds the archives keep.\n\n1. Load the world at the iteration and play one game, so its messages are fresh.\n2. Draw the random inputs 8 times and turn every brain's outputs into stakes as the engine does: the others' play, as 8 draws.\n3. For up to 300 agents with three tokens or more, score its own play and the best of a set of replies (everything home; the cheapest way to keep its node and take the cheapest neighbours; taking without defending) by the nodes it holds after the game, averaged over the 8 draws (`python3 research/oee/checks/exploitability.py RUN:ITERATION \u2026 --draws 8 --sample 300`).\n4. The gain is the best reply's score less the own play's, averaged over the agents.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py eras`."
  }
